            <ArticleTitle>Virtual-screening workflow tutorials and prospective results from the Teach-Discover-Treat competition 2014 against malaria.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1136</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.12688/f1000research.11905.1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The first challenge in the 2014 competition launched by the Teach-Discover-Treat (TDT) initiative asked for the development of a tutorial for ligand-based virtual screening, based on data from a primary phenotypic high-throughput screen (HTS) against malaria. The resulting Workflows were applied to select compounds from a commercial database, and a subset of those were purchased and tested experimentally for anti-malaria activity. Here, we present the two most successful Workflows, both using machine-learning approaches, and report the results for the 114 compounds tested in the follow-up screen. Excluding the two known anti-malarials quinidine and amodiaquine and 31 compounds already present in the primary HTS, a high hit rate of 57% was found.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
